<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Gage</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1719–<a href="/years/1787">1787</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Gage was a British army officer who served as commander-in-chief in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> from 1763 to 1775 and as the last royal governor of Massachusetts from 1774. His attempt to seize colonial military stores at Concord led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battles-of-lexington-and-concord">Battles of Lexington and Concord</a> in April 1775, and the costly victory at Bunker Hill followed in June. He was recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> later that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-gage.jpg" alt="Thomas Gage" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Singleton Copley · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_Gage_John_Singleton_Copley.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Thomas Gage was born at Firle in Sussex, the second of three children of Thomas Gage, 1st Viscount Gage, whose family had been seated at Firle Place since the 15th century. The family was long Catholic, but his father had adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Anglican Church</a> in 1715, and Gage became firmly attached to it, developing a dislike of the Roman Catholic Church. He attended Westminster School from 1728, where he met John Burgoyne, Richard Howe and George Germain.</p><p>In 1755 his regiment went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> with General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-braddock">Edward Braddock</a>, and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-monongahela">Battle of the Monongahela</a>, where Braddock was mortally wounded and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> distinguished himself, Gage was slightly wounded. In December 1757 he proposed a light infantry regiment suited to woodland warfare, and recruited the 80th Regiment of Light-Armed Foot. He married Margaret Kemble on 8 December 1758.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the French surrender in 1760, Jeffery Amherst named him military governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, where he proved a fair administrator. He took over as commander-in-chief in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> on 17 November 1763 and inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontiac">Pontiac</a>'s War, which he sought to end by diplomacy through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-johnson">Sir William Johnson</a> as well as by expeditions under Colonel John Bradstreet and Colonel Henry Bouquet.</p><p>As colonial tension rose he withdrew troops from the frontier to cities, and in 1768 he recommended sending two regiments to occupy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, which further inflamed the city. In 1772 he wrote that democracy was too prevalent in America. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in June 1773, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston-tea-party">Boston Tea Party</a> he was thought the best man to enforce the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intolerable-acts">Intolerable Acts</a> as governor of Massachusetts Bay.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Orders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 14 April 1775 led him to send troops to seize militia stores at Concord on the night of 18 April; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battles-of-lexington-and-concord">Battles of Lexington and Concord</a> cost the British 273 casualties and the colonists 93. After the Battle of Bunker Hill on 17 June, ministers recalled him, replaced him with William Howe, and he sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on 11 October 1775.</p><p>He retained the governorship of Massachusetts, but some writers about him were vicious, while New Hampshire Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benning-wentworth">Benning Wentworth</a> called him a good and wise man surrounded by difficulties. He died at Portland Place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 2 April 1787 and was buried at Firle. Gagetown in New Brunswick is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thomas Gage” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Massachusetts Government Act of 1774, General <b>Thomas Gage</b> ruled, and fighting at Lexington and Concord in 1775 began the war that made Massachusetts a state of the United States.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-massachusetts-bay">Province of Massachusetts Bay</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Firle, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Portland Place, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.85,0.09&amp;year=1763" aria-label="Firle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4552 400 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Thomas Gage"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5002 670h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5002 670h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.85,0.09&amp;year=1763">What was here in 1763? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
